#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231788} :activity/id #uuid "ada4450f-fa33-5c1f-88fc-47bac91ee61e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050814 :topic/id #uuid "68ad7925-6faa-546c-899e-dac0bbfac1ef" :topic/math-academy-id 6722} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231986 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will use Fourier series to solve boundary value problems with periodic forcing functions.\n\nAs before, we consider a second-order linear differential equation with constant coefficients and forcing function $f(x)$, for which the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe main ideas are similar to those of solving IVPs:\n\n- we choose the correct form of the series for the particular solution $y_p$, and then\n\n- use boundary conditions at the endpoints to determine the constants in the homogeneous part $y_c$ of the solution.\n\nWhen $f(x)$ is periodic, we often look for $y_p$ as a Fourier series on a symmetric interval $[-L,L].$\n\nRecall that for an equation of the form $y''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)$ with no (damping) $y'$ term, the particular solution inherits the parity of $f(x)$:\n\n- If $f$ is *even*, then its Fourier series contains only cosine terms.\n\n- If $f$ is *odd*, then its Fourier series contains only sine terms.\n\nFor example, consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny''+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and odd. We want the appropriate Fourier series form for a particular solution $y_p$.\n\nBecause $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so the Fourier basis on this interval is\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. Since the equation has no $y'$ term, the particular solution inherits the same parity and is also odd.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for $y_p$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "67a0ca11-7450-5f6a-8bdc-4aee6301f209" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14775 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "46d5d3ac-e51c-5772-ac47-f492d00757d3" :step/math-academy-id 601823 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231296 :question/id #uuid "f4a74f28-4b3b-5602-b5e5-53c8590d4a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-21887"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050730}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516819}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"} :step/id #uuid "68b0c11a-9b61-56e1-82c7-12c59242211e" :step/math-academy-id 601824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231292 :question/id #uuid "b71e74ab-9cb3-5e43-94aa-52c2a926dc5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21874"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049324}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508963}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "23fc6608-1b88-5376-be85-f016f7a7fd7f" :step/math-academy-id 601827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231992 :tutorial/content "Recall that we are considering a second-order linear differential equation with constant coefficients and forcing function $f(x)$, for which the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nThere are many types of boundary conditions (including mixed or Robin-type conditions), but in this lesson, we will focus on the two simplest cases:\n\n- *Dirichlet conditions* (values of $y$ are specified):\n\n$$\ny(a)=\\alpha\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny(b)=\\beta\n$$\n\n- *Neumann conditions* (values of $y'$ are specified):\n\n$$\ny'(a)=\\alpha\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny'(b)=\\beta\n$$\n\nA key simplification is possible if we choose our particular solution $y_p$ to satisfy the corresponding *homogeneous* boundary conditions. For any given $f(x),$ it is always possible to find such a $y_p$. \nThis drastically simplifies the computations, as the boundary conditions then determine the constants in $y_c$ directly.\n\n- *Dirichlet-type simplification:* If we choose the forcing so that\n\n$$\ny_p(a)=0\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_p(b)=0,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ny(a)=y_c(a)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny(b)=y_c(b),\n$$\n\nso we can solve for the constants in $y_c$ using only the homogeneous solution.\n\n- *Neumann-type simplification:* If we choose the forcing so that\n\n$$\ny_p'(a)=0\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_p'(b)=0,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ny'(a)=y_c'(a)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny'(b)=y_c'(b),\n$$\n\nso the derivative conditions determine the constants in $y_c$ directly.\n\nLet's see how this works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "16e90dd1-2074-5728-ad7b-e05a00c3e171" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14795 :tutorial/title "Using Boundary Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "46704bc7-b368-569c-9ed3-f228bcfd99b9" :step/math-academy-id 601983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231790}}] :activity/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231522}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497436} :activity/id #uuid "90858bbd-4be9-59a1-90ae-3a0211b98927" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050814 :topic/id #uuid "68ad7925-6faa-546c-899e-dac0bbfac1ef" :topic/math-academy-id 6722} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462d45d4-2587-5767-bbda-82dc90eb9b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496809} {:answer/id #uuid "480be999-cd10-5a4f-83f5-02eb7f7b0275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496819} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1a635-ce6b-5e3d-a212-6ca66dbb9f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0f4e40-8515-583c-aa83-8de5f9788688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a94d15-fe13-5d85-9f7d-69d7a8a6ed69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed7b5966-6097-5c94-afc7-cde3e72271b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344323" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0664f390-8c5d-5111-9629-741058ac23ee"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497436 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cafa24e-4543-5b34-90d7-30a196dea194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372868} {:answer/id #uuid "e11913eb-e3e1-5f57-a56a-942b4c1c6e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372869} {:answer/id #uuid "677af0cd-8fcf-515a-9ae8-5589651ec3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372870} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca9f35e-fe96-5630-bbab-c22309b7bc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372868} :answer-field/id #uuid "426794d2-6862-5438-ba72-af2a254821f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28c90549-a363-5df9-b9c5-c5f06fe88f4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344281" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-1,1]$, where $f$ is $2$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $2$-periodic on $[-1,1]$, we have $L=1$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{1})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{1}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5219f94f-df72-53fc-a578-2400b0bec682" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca1a5ef5-d795-51cc-943c-7a342a6b79ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372886} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d3a205-6dc6-5c9e-bac4-a249e8ab4de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372887} {:answer/id #uuid "2853c5b9-37c6-5227-b945-7d1f193fd0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372888} {:answer/id #uuid "83ee3b8e-a567-507f-a915-c7357bb35a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372889} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b894e19-8672-5d3a-ad64-9899ae78c522" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1435ee5-1c1b-550d-94b4-e30eac47db5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344322" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+8y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{8}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{8}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 3 & =-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8acff912-5281-5c53-9ef5-b6eb39e2fc94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497420}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "850e4194-b453-541e-be6e-32d1ccb2f133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496930} {:answer/id #uuid "88cf4171-cb9b-50eb-bbf6-945c7832695e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496942} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c84257-de9c-5c9c-a2d5-4b7b4af017c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496993} {:answer/id #uuid "fbab4fd2-4962-567f-9b94-4d8fec410878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496993} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8624c8-93b0-5430-b609-5a1fb6fd1e74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b4d6e1d-9198-53a2-8fea-90e3eb2a7b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-344316" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+3y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=2$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 2 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7df0d1a-8d1c-5391-8fd4-541fd7cfa273" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bcde23-8fb4-54be-b4f9-12b3458b95b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496665} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8a9980-7371-5df1-8410-26fa8e6ff9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496683} {:answer/id #uuid "1989b09c-b976-5029-b925-43a8bcf85f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} {:answer/id #uuid "86a2fd99-6f60-54d4-ba6c-934c44c7e141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d46346-4c74-57e4-aaa3-613d6a4a40ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f217825-c491-59e7-9325-ea7e5e5d43ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-344285" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, where $f$ is $3$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $3$-periodic on $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, we have $L=\\frac{3}{2}$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc86aaa6-2771-57b6-8f41-5b9ad203d185" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497482}}] :activity/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496654}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509012} :activity/id #uuid "96850cf5-d4b6-549d-855c-af0332a0f248" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050814 :topic/id #uuid "68ad7925-6faa-546c-899e-dac0bbfac1ef" :topic/math-academy-id 6722} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e980a7-73e5-5db5-b117-dad8065a2ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508401} {:answer/id #uuid "7899c111-a756-5f84-bb7b-8a3a2fc1f66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508565} {:answer/id #uuid "846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508588} {:answer/id #uuid "aff8259e-8a40-5b92-873a-8c1bbf4d08f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508588} :answer-field/id #uuid "820b3701-ddd6-58d9-9b7a-77aa5711efbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ccc73a6-d864-55e4-9da3-f78dd465c648" :question/math-academy-id "q-343671" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3cad45fa-b76b-5d49-807e-179e9cf7aed9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a6be11-8878-5070-81b7-b47676c4ce8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385292} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce9062b-d93f-55c2-a03a-1ae8c327c26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385293} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa04091-bc19-5979-8a5e-2e3897f35181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385294} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee0778f-6531-5ab8-bddf-60696be3a1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385295} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ecdd4ec-27ca-5ae4-9688-c8b8aa4f79ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6a08e9ea-1ab7-5988-bf4e-d55c1dd407e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343668" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=3$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 3 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "571e314f-a98c-570d-9438-f25fd9bdb86f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1ee564-8e0f-5ee0-998f-2101fc9cd57a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372880} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1903c2-0865-5c88-ad0e-67617d3c44ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372881} {:answer/id #uuid "6005d9a8-46b9-5819-acc2-87a742a05929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372882} {:answer/id #uuid "3078353a-b59d-5067-b6f6-a1a830986836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372883} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f8df2d-ecbb-512a-aa1b-2ac650da1f39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9b96aedd-ce80-5f05-9df5-d9873f821b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-344321" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=4$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 4 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "817024dc-13d6-502b-82e0-d9f5442ed8f4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1db83d-b0ff-520c-a225-6d525cff002b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372856} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8ea9a4-c740-500c-9fab-6d37ceab5899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372857} {:answer/id #uuid "7e24b0bc-eb98-5df7-b3c2-9e5493a8cc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372858} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6e5693-da89-597a-8638-edeca9949654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372858} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e4c22e-78c5-5e23-8e3c-f648b14c9d5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31df3052-c15b-5b42-8dc7-4a21ca73beb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344267" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-4,4]$, where $f$ is $8$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $8$-periodic on $[-4,4]$, we have $L=4$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c8eee27f-b98e-539a-b55c-614e4d95051f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db0a3b8-eaf2-565b-a867-fb89aa025843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508605} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7031cc-3266-50a5-b23e-e80324be569a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508652} {:answer/id #uuid "b5605cf5-4e40-5482-98b8-1d348f6bdb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508660} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508687} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b8581d-d97f-5540-9faf-641daf95fba0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-344320" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de0f6281-e792-51d2-a694-b1b34794e517" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509018}}] :activity/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508383}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516893} :activity/id #uuid "6ade1ba4-a844-5f17-ae51-ad9fb452b45a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050814 :topic/id #uuid "68ad7925-6faa-546c-899e-dac0bbfac1ef" :topic/math-academy-id 6722} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00db171e-86f4-578f-baa9-eeb4a13a294d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516113} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7a31b2-8edf-5725-b08a-4ca04a19f52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516267} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc33023-61a9-57c1-b84d-42d2debd3175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516405} {:answer/id #uuid "8eef8445-476b-5036-b260-03f7d53b7715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516411}] :answer-field/correc